      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The borough’s name derives from the French “mont d’or,” meaning “Mountain of Gold,” a nod to its hilltop setting.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dormont%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Dormont Historical Society, founded in March 1999, preserves the borough’s memories and hosts a photo gallery and calendar.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        11,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dormonthistoricalsociety.org/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Every fall, Dormont hosts the Street and Music Festival, featuring two stages and bands from 2 p.m. to 10 p.m.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dormont%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Dormont’s municipal building, located between Glenmore and Espy Avenues, also houses the Historical Society.",
